  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWith a surface high limiting mechanical mixing and this mornings \nsounding at MPX showing a strong inversion, stratus has been slow to \nbreak up today. This has really limited temperature rises today, \nwith most of the area seeing highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s, or 5 \nto 10 degrees below normal. Water vapor imagery and h5 analysis this \nafternoon shows us what our next concern is and that is the two \nwaves heading our way. The northern stream wave is a shortwave \ncoming across Alberta, with a southern stream wave in the form of an \nh5 low over the 4-corners. These two features will phase Thursday \nover the central CONUS, causing a surface low to deepen. This \nsurface low will be a panhandle hook type system, with the southern \nstream wave driving a surface low from the TX panhandle to northern \nIllinois during the day on Thursday into Thursday night. There will \nbe a healthy deformation band of precip to the northwest of the low \ntrack, with the heaviest precip falling across Iowa into southern \nWI. Still, the northern edge of this precipitation shield will come \ninto southern MN, which will setup the southeast to northwest \ngradient in rainfall amounts we're expecting. We've seen a slight \nuptick in precip amounts along that Albert Lea to Eau Claire line, \nwith amounts up around 0.75\" depicted on the REFS. This falls to \naround 0.25\" in the Twin Cities, a couple hundredths in St. Cloud, \nand nothing at Alexandria (until Thursday night with the potential \nfor wrap around precip in the strong CAA regime). Besides the rain, \nthe northern stream will bring in some strong CAA Thursday night, \nwith wind gusts upwards of 45mph out of the northwest possible out \nin our typical windy areas of west central through south central MN.\n\nFollowing this will come a quick blast of cooler air as a 1040mb \nhigh comes out of the Rockies. This will set us up for our coldest \nmorning of the Fall so far on Saturday morning. This will be our \nfirst widespread freeze of the season outside of the core of the \nTwin Cities urban metro area. Even for the Twin Cities, this will \nlikely be the first time the low drops below 40 this season. For a \nfreeze, we're in the ballpark of the 90th percentile for a late \nfreeze (so 90% of falls have seen a first freeze earlier than this). \nFor MSP, the latest we've had our first sub-40 reading in the fall \nwas October 17th back in 1931, which also happens to be the day \nSaturday falls on this year, so why lot tack on the latest first sub-\n40 low of the Fall on to the latest 90 on record for this October.\n\nAfter Friday, we're back into ridging and mild southerly winds. In \nfact, the NBM has southerly winds of some variety remaining in place \nfrom Saturday through Wednesday. This will setup another stretch of \nvery pleasant fall weather. H85 temperatures will be running up \naround +10C starting Sunday, with large swaths of the MPX area \nlikely to see highs in the low to mid 70s starting Sunday and likely \nlasting through much of next week. As for the end of the week, \nmodels have been hinting at a stronger system moving across the \ncentral CONUS. It's still there, but to no surprise, we're seeing \nthe models slowing down the demise of our ridge, with that next \nfropa and chances for more widespread precipitation now getting \npushed out toward next weekend (24th/25th). Given what we've had so \nfar and the fact that the only sign of cold air this month is what \nwe'll see Thursday night through Friday night, it's certainly \nlooking more plausible that the warmest October on record could be \nin play this year.",
